{"body": "No returned account is present in the supplied material—the “Returned account” line is empty—so I base the following only on the stipulated case data.\n\n## Case A\n\nUnder the reporting specification:\n\n\\[\n6+5=11,\\qquad 9-11=-2.\n\\]\n\nSo the reporting result is **-2**.\n\nWhat follows is limited to the stipulated calculation: the selected quantities sum to 11, which exceeds the stated capacity 9 by 2, so the calculated remaining integer is negative. It does **not** follow that either selected entry is individually invalid: 6 is not greater than 9, and 5 is not greater than 9. It also does not follow which entry, if any, should be changed, released, or rejected.\n\nA stronger conclusion, such as “both holds cannot remain active together” or “one hold must be released,” needs an additional premise. At minimum, one must assume that these active holds are simultaneous claims on the same hard capacity and that the capacity cannot be exceeded. The reporting calculation alone gives an arithmetic result, not a normative conclusion about the entries.\n\n## Case B\n\nThe snapshot procedure forms all four pairs:\n\n| U choice | V choice | Calculation | Reporting result |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 0 | 0 | \\(23-0-0\\) | 23 |\n| 0 | 11 | \\(23-0-11\\) | 12 |\n| 8 | 0 | \\(23-8-0\\) | 15 |\n| 8 | 11 | \\(23-8-11\\) | 4 |\n\nSo the specified reporting procedure gives the values:\n\n\\[\n23,\\ 12,\\ 15,\\ 4.\n\\]\n\nWith the distinct inspection file, the pair \\((8,11)\\) cannot be performed jointly because U=8 and V=11 both need the same single-use jig at the same time. Each other listed pair can be performed jointly. Therefore the planner’s requested values for jointly performable pairs are:\n\n| Performable pair | Calculation | Planner value |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| (0,0) | \\(23-0-0\\) | 23 |\n| (0,11) | \\(23-0-11\\) | 12 |\n| (8,0) | \\(23-8-0\\) | 15 |\n\nThe value 4 remains the arithmetic reporting output for \\((8,11)\\), but it is not a value for a jointly performable pair under the inspection file.\n\nA reader with only the snapshot and reporting procedure can support the four arithmetic outputs above and can note that every listed pair’s combined quantity is at most \\(8+11=19\\), which is below capacity 23, so every arithmetic residual is nonnegative. That reader cannot determine joint performability. In particular, that reader cannot rule out \\((8,11)\\), cannot rule in \\((8,11)\\), and cannot conclude that all listed pairs are jointly performable. Those remain open without the inspection file or another resource/scheduling source.\n\n## The reviewer’s remark before inspection\n\nThe reviewer wrote that each listed quantity may be defensible on its own, yet a pairing might fail to be jointly performable, and that an unnamed resource might matter. At the time it was made, this supplies no observed resource and no identified impossible pair. It is therefore best read as a caution about inferential completeness, not as evidence for a particular numerical change.\n\nIt challenges commitments such as:\n\n1. that individual defensibility of listed quantities implies joint performability of pairs;\n2. that capacity arithmetic is sufficient for feasibility;\n3. that the snapshot’s fields exhaust all relevant resources;\n4. that the reporting outputs can be used directly as feasible option values.\n\nWhere an account uses the snapshot only to report arithmetic, the remark does not force a change. Where an account uses the snapshot to claim “these are the feasible pairs” or “all four residuals are available plans,” the remark challenges that use.\n\nDefensible alternative readings include:\n\n- **Weak modal reading:** there merely might be a hidden conflict; nothing specific is asserted. This is compatible with the inspection file later confirming one conflict, but the remark alone does not identify it.\n- **Strong substantive reading:** there really is some unlisted resource constraint that matters. Even then, the remark alone does not say which pair or resource, so it still cannot justify changing a listed quantity or excluding a specific pair.\n- **Methodological reading:** it challenges the model’s completeness, not any of the listed numbers.\n\nA case that would distinguish these readings is exactly the kind of inspection file supplied for Case B: it names a shared single-use jig and identifies \\((8,11)\\) as impossible. That confirms a specific hidden constraint. Conversely, an exhaustive resource audit showing no shared-resource conflicts would support the weak reading while weakening the strong reading.\n\nNothing in the remark alone requires changing U’s listed quantities \\(0,8\\), V’s listed quantities \\(0,11\\), capacity 23, or the calculation \\(23-u-v\\). It may require adding caveats or obtaining evidence before treating the arithmetic outputs as feasibility results.\n\n## Relation to the numerical answers\n\nThe reporting answers for Case B remain \\(23,12,15,4\\). The planner’s answers, when the inspection file is available, are \\(23,12,15\\). The difference is not a change in capacity or in the formula; it is a change in the question: the planner asks only about jointly performable pairs.\n\nThe reviewer’s remark, before inspection, explains why a snapshot-only reader should not automatically transfer the arithmetic value 4 for \\((8,11)\\) into a feasible-plan value. But it is the inspection file, not the remark alone, that supplies the specific ground for excluding \\((8,11)\\). So the reviewer’s warning and the planner’s exclusion are related in spirit, but they have different evidential strength. The unresolved issue, outside this finite case, is whether other unlisted constraints affect other pairs; within Case B the inspection file stipulates that each other listed pair can be performed jointly.", "commitments": "No returned account is present in the supplied material—the “Returned account” line is empty—so I base the following only on the stipulated case d